WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.904
Why public benchmarks don’t tell you how a model does on your task.

2
00:00:04.184 --> 00:00:10.691
Leaderboards are where most people first meet “evaluation,” and Studio 1 already showed you their caveats.

3
00:00:10.971 --> 00:00:13.061
Here is the summary in eval terms.

4
00:00:13.341 --> 00:00:17.117
A public benchmark is a fixed set of tasks with a fixed grader.

5
00:00:17.397 --> 00:00:24.309
Your task has different inputs, different rules (your refund policy, your escalation list), and different failure modes.

6
00:00:24.589 --> 00:00:37.453
Chip Huyen’s AI Engineering treats public benchmarks as one input to model selection (chapter 4 has a section on navigating them), alongside evaluating candidates on your own application.

7
00:00:37.733 --> 00:00:45.221
The reason is distribution: a benchmark tells you how a model does on the benchmark’s inputs, and your inputs are not those.

8
00:00:45.501 --> 00:00:50.109
Three specific problems make benchmark numbers weaker evidence than they look.

9
00:00:50.389 --> 00:01:02.784
Contamination: Deng and colleagues (2023) found signs that benchmark content had leaked into training data for some models, which inflates scores without reflecting real ability.

10
00:01:03.064 --> 00:01:16.717
Leaderboard practices: Singh and colleagues (2025) documented ways the Chatbot Arena leaderboard’s rules let some providers test many private variants and report the best, which distorts rankings.

11
00:01:16.997 --> 00:01:33.765
Grading bugs: Anthropic’s evals post reports that one model’s score on the CORE-Bench benchmark rose from 42 to 95 percent after the team fixed grading bugs and loosened a constrained scaffold, so the original number measured the harness as much as the model.

12
00:01:34.045 --> 00:01:36.115
None of this makes benchmarks useless.

13
00:01:36.395 --> 00:01:40.853
The evals FAQ says they can help you choose a promising model to start with.

14
00:01:41.133 --> 00:01:54.936
That is the right role: shortlist two or three models (say, a Claude, a GPT, and an open-weight model you could self-host) using benchmarks, price, and latency, then let an eval built from your traffic pick the winner.

15
00:01:55.216 --> 00:02:00.549
The same eval then tells you, months later, whether a new model release is worth switching to.
